PENDING: revision 20 (27 FRs / 10 NFRs / 51 ACs / 122 normative bullets) — NOT YET APPROVED.

Revision 20 was made during the design phase, in response to round 5 of the design review, and
carries design decision 4 of that round. It is NEW SCOPE, not alignment, and so it owes a
requirements review - but NOT one of its own, and NOT now.

THE OWNER CHANGED THE PROCESS AT THIS POINT, and the change governs every later revision too:
requirements changes arising from design-review findings are ACCUMULATED and reviewed ONCE, in a
single true-up at the end of the design phase, rather than each triggering its own round. The
reason is that reviewing per change means reviewing text that is still moving - the design round
that produced this change is not finished, and more requirements changes are likely - so a
per-change round responds to flux and would be re-run against different text a few commits later.
The standing alignment-vs-more-than-alignment rule is unchanged in substance; what changes is WHEN
the round for a more-than-alignment change is run. PROMPT.md section 18.8 is the running ledger of
what the true-up must cover.

What it added: AC-51, the acceptance criterion for FR-13's handler-release half. ADR 0071 makes a
handler-factory Release that throws logged at Error and swallowed rather than propagating - the one
behavioural change that ADR calls observable to an application - and round 5 found the ADR citing
AC-7 as its regression guard. AC-7's Given has a throwing HANDLER, not a throwing Release; it
guards FR-6's release-exactly-once rule and says nothing about a failing teardown. So the rule had
no criterion at all, and the mis-citation concealed the gap rather than declaring it.

AC-51 has four clauses: the release failure is logged at Error and the caller's normal result is
unchanged; a handler that throws has ITS OWN exception reach the caller rather than the release
failure or an AggregateException composing the two (the clause that fails the natural wrong
implementation, since the builder is disposed under `using var` and a rethrowing teardown replaces
the handler's exception - FR-5 and FR-6 forbid it); a Release throwing on the first of three
tracked handlers still releases the other two and still disposes the scope, with exactly one Error
record; and the failure is not latched.

AC-51 is the sibling of AC-33, which covers the same requirement's scope-disposal half. No FR, NFR
or constraint changes, and no existing AC changes. Counts move from 50 ACs to 51; FRs, NFRs and
normative bullets are unchanged at 27/10/122.

Why it owes a review, and why not yet: the standing rule is that alignment needs none and an
amendment that is more than alignment gets one. This adds a criterion that did not exist and
imposes new obligations on the implementation, so it is more than alignment and owes one. Under
the process change above it is carried to the end-of-phase true-up instead of being reviewed on its
own. Round-2's revision 16 - which added FR-22.4 and AC-50 and had its own round - remains the
precedent for the RULE; the change is only to when the round runs.

Approved: revision 19, 2026-08-06 (27 FRs / 10 NFRs / 50 ACs / 122 normative bullets).

Revision 19 was made during the design phase, in response to round 5 of the design review, and
carries design decision 2 of that round. It is ALIGNMENT, not new scope, and was approved without
an independent requirements review round under the requirement owner's standing rule.

What it changed: FR-9(ii) named TWO ways of leaving the caller suppressed after Publish returns -
establishing suppression around the loop in the caller's own flow, OR setting it inside a
Parallel.ForEach body and never restoring it - and said AC-39's final clauses detect both. The
round-5 reviewer of ADR 0075 built and ran a thirteen-case .NET probe. The second disjunct is
mechanically FALSE: the runtime captures and restores ExecutionContext per REPLICA, including the
replica Parallel.ForEach inlines on the calling thread, so a body-level write never reaches the
caller. The probe measured a run in which the calling thread executed SEVEN of the bodies and the
caller still read IsSuppressed = False.

FR-9's rule, both brackets, their placement, their restore-on-every-exit-path obligation, AC-39 and
AC-12 are ALL UNCHANGED, and no AC changes at all. Only the false disjunct is removed: FR-9(ii) now
states that the caller-flow failure mode is the bracket's PLACEMENT, that a body-level write is
confined to the worker, and that it therefore shows up only as FR-9(i)'s unobservable cross-body
leak. The Design notes paragraph carried the same false claim in its fourth failure mode and is
corrected to match - that mode is now stated as not detectable at all, which is what FR-9(i)
already implied and what the probe establishes. FR-9(ii)'s reference to AC-39's clauses is also
spelled as "leak clauses", matching AC-39's own wording; AC-39's actual final clause is the
no-ordering, no-overlap constraint.

Why no review round: the standing rule is that alignment needs none and an amendment that is more
than alignment gets one. This adds no rule, no AC and no scope; it removes an empirically false
claim about the .NET runtime and leaves every normative obligation in place. Counts unchanged at
27/10/50/122. The probe output is recorded in review-design.md (reviewer 0075, finding 1, score 70,
and the Verification log's probe section).

